  "decision_conditions": [
    "Insufficient and inadequate information has been provided to ensure that the proposed development would achieve the statutory 10% Biodiversity Net Gain (BNG) requirement. It is noted that recent substantive tree clearance has occurred at the site that could be considered to be 'pre-emptive clearance' and it is unclear as to whether the baseline figure provided demonstrates the current situation or the situation prior to the substantive tree clearance that has occurred. As such the 'precautionary principle' would need to be adopted in the BNG calculation metrics. In addition to this, in the absence of an appropriate legal agreement to secure off-site provision of BNG and the required 30-year maintenance period, the proposal would result in a net loss of biodiversity within the site. The proposal would accordingly fail to comply with Schedule 7A of the Town and Country Planning Act 1990 (as amended), Policy G6 of the London Plan, the NPPF and Policy DM6 of the Kingston Core Strategy.",
    "The proposed dwelling, annotated as House 3, by reason of its bulk, scale, height, appearance, excessive footprint and proximity to boundaries, would constitute an incongruous and discordant form of development, that would fail to provide a visually attractive form of development that would fail to be consistent with the prevailing pattern of development and would not by sympathetic to the surrounding built form and its landscape setting, to the detriment of local character. The proposal would accordingly fail to deliver good design and would fail to comply with Paragraph 135 of the NPPF, Policy D3 of the London Plan, Policies DM10 and CS8 of the Kingston Core Strategy and the Kingston Residential Design SPD.",
    "The proposed dwelling, annotated as House 3, due to its spatial arrangement within the plot and the presence of vertical louvres to all first floor windows, would result in a substandard form of residential accommodation, that would offer insufficient outlook to future occupants, resulting in a unreasonable sense of enclosure, to the detriment of the living conditions of future residents. The proposal would accordingly fail to comply with Policy D6 of the London Plan and the Kingston Residential Design SPD.",
    "The proposed dwelling, annotated as House 3, would provide excessive off-street parking provision and contrived access arrangements, that would exceed the maximum standards outlined by Policy T6.1 of the London Plan. The proposal would therefore create a car-centric form of development that would not encourage a modal shift to more sustainable forms of transportation. In addition, in the absence of an executed Unilateral Undertaking to ensure that the development is car capped i.e. current and/or future occupants of the development would not be able to obtain car parking permits, the proposed development would be highly likely to lead to inconsiderate parking on surrounding streets, which would, in turn, compromise highway safety. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policy T4 of the London Plan 2021 and policy DM10 of the LDF Core Strategy 2012.",
    "The submitted Arboricultural Method Statement and Tree Protection Plan, by virtue of the use of estimated stem measurements, would provide potentially inaccurate representations of the Root Protection Area of the TPO trees in the near vicinity of the site. The submitted information therefore fails to adequately demonstrate that the proposed development would not have a material impact on the longevity and health of these trees. The proposal would accordingly fail to comply with Policy DM10 of the Kingston Core Strategy, Chapter 15 of the NPPF and Policy G7 of the London Plan"
  ],
